  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 318 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThere is increasing confidence in greater chances of precipitation \non Saturday evening into Sunday morning. Increased moisture will \nsurge into the region as the mid level Atlantic trough or cut off \nlow continues to dig south into the western Atlantic. Some solutions \ndisplay this system stalling as far south as Cuba, with a stationary \nfrontal boundary at the surface draped across South Florida. \nHowever, precipitable water values don't seem all too impressive, \nwith values climbing up to around 1.6 to 1.7 inches, which is \nbetween the mean and 75th percentile for this time of year. So while \nchances of precipitation will be high, between 85% and 95% for \nthe Atlantic waters and coastal Atlantic areas, QPF continues to \ntrend down among guidance. Ensemble QPF clusters vary both \nspatially and temporally due to the uncertainty regarding the \nupper low feature. Some solutions keep the highest QPF offshore, \nwhile others bring it onshore but north of South Florida. One \nscenario brings a swath of heavier precipitation along the eastern\nmetro areas, between 2 to 3 inches through Monday. Model \nsoundings indicate that conditions will be favorable for coastal \nconvergence storms to be pinned along the coast, with brisk \nnortheasterly flow at the surface nearly opposite to the strong \nwesterly to southwesterly flow in the 700 mb and 500 mb heights. \nSo, if several storms were to be pinned along the coast for \nseveral hours, localized urban flooding for low lying and poor \ndrainage areas may be possible. However, chances of widespread \namounts of greater than 2 inches are only at around 45%, with \nhighest rain amounts likely staying over the waters.\n\nStrong northeast winds will persist through Monday and some \nlocations near the coast will be flirting with Wind Advisory \nconditions. Broken tree branches and loose objects may be flung \nabout in winds between 25 to 35 mph. One thing to keep in mind \nfor this weekend is the combination of the King Tide with heavy \nrain and increased onshore flow. All these factors together may \nraise tide and water levels into the moderate flood category for \ncoastal urban areas along Dade, Broward, and Palm Beach. Roadways \nand low lying areas may be flooded for several hours, especially \nif the high tide coincides with heavy rainfall. Remember, turn \naround, don't drown. Never drive, walk, or wade, over flooded \nareas. \n\nSurface high pressure over the northeast will keep the tight \npressure gradient along the eastern seaboard through the middle of \nthe week. This will allow for breezy winds and coastal showers to \ncontinue through at least Wednesday. Chances of precipitation  drop \nto around 40% for eastern parts of South Florida through the end of \nthe week as mid-level ridging begins to build over the Gulf and\nslide over Florida.",
